	doi = {10.1109/CICC65509.2026.11509468},
	abstract = {Non-Volatile SRAM (NVSRAM) integrates non-volatile memory (NVM) into conventional SRAM to provide fast access and non-volatility within a unified architecture. This work presents the first demonstration of an NVSRAM-based Physical Unclonable Function (PUF) implemented in a 28 nm RRAM-integrated CMOS process. A compact 0.612 µm2 10T2R cell and {\textgreater}500 MHz measured SRAM speed both achieve state-of-the-art performance among fabricated NVSRAM macros, enabling reliable and high-speed PUF operation. During PUF enrollment, an in-situ resistance-split process yields a low native BER of 3.03×10-6, which is further reduced to 1.15×10-25 through a novel row-level error correction code (ECC) with only 12.5\% redundancy. The NVSRAM-PUF reaches a 16.32 Gbit/s bit rate and demonstrates excellent PUF properties, including near-ideal uniformity, uniqueness, diffuseness and randomness.},
