	doi = {10.1109/VLSISATA69163.2026.11610523},
	abstract = {Physically Unclonable Functions (PUFs) prove useful for secure identification and secret-key generation due to manufacturing variations. But traditional PUF architectures based on CMOS technologies may suffer from low voltage and temperature tolerance, and lack flexibility and stability over time due to the static nature of their response. In this paper, we therefore present a reconfigurable and low-power PUF design using CNTFET. CNTFETs and delay and sensing blocks use natural nanoscale process variations to enhance entropy and low energy consumption while taking advantage of low leakage features. An adjustable delay-cell design facilitates reconfiguration to obtain multiple trusted identities without secret storage. Simulation results demonstrate efficient and reliable response generation with around 70-80\% reduction in power consumption compared to CMOS-based PUFs. Through variability analysis using inter-die Hamming distances of around ideal 50\%, we also demonstrate good uniqueness, reliability and security, making this architecture an ideal fit for secure, energy-efficient IoT, and embedded devices.},
