	doi = {10.1109/PICC67314.2025.11291381},
	abstract = {Security is very important in Vehicular Ad-Hoc Networks (VANETs) to protect messages from being tampered with or stolen during communication. This paper proposes a lightweight and secure key agreement framework that combines classical cryptographic key exchange methods with Physically Unclonable Function (PUF)-based key derivation. The system uses protocols such as DH, ECDH, DHE, ECDHE and EDEDH to generate a shared 256 -bit secret, which is then combined with a unique 128-bit PUF-generated key using XOR and SHA256 hashing to form the final session key. The framework enhances security by making the key device-specific and resistant to physical tampering. Performance is evaluated using four metrics: Hamming distance, Shannon entropy, bit agreement and execution time. Results show that all schemes generate highentropy, unpredictable keys, while also maintaining low latency suitable for constrained devices. A {\textbackslash}mathbf5 0 \% bit agreement in man-in-the-middle (MITM) attack scenarios confirms the system's ability to detect interference. Among the evaluated protocols, Enhanced DHE (EDEDH) demonstrates a balanced performance in terms of security strength and computational efficiency, indicating its suitability for secure communication in resource-constrained environments.},
