	abstract = {This paper presents the design of a high-reliability True Random Number Generator (TRNG), which consists of a ring oscillator block that incorporates a Von Neumann corrector, a dual-clock First-In First-Out (FIFO) block, a real-time entropy monitor that uses binary Shannon entropy for early fault detection in the entropy source, and two FSMs. To validate the reliability of the proposed TRNG, the repetition count test (RCT) and the adaptive proportion test (APT), as recommended by NIST SP 800-90B, were carried out. Additionally, a Universal Asynchronous Receiver-Transmitter (UART) interface was implemented to transmit the bits generated by the TRNG to a PC. TRNG was synthesized on a Field-Programmable Gate Array (FPGA) device (Cyclone V 5CSEBA6U23IE), evaluated using the NIST 800-22 statistical test suite, and verified in hardware using the DE10-Nano board and the Signal Tap tool. The synthesis results show that TRNG exhibits good performance with a low area resource requirement. According to the results, TRNG achieves a throughput of {\textbackslash}mathbf5 0. 7 5 M b p s with a measured entropy of 0.9959 bits/bit, utilizing 274 LUTs, 24-kbit of ROM, and consuming 224.36 mW of power.},
